WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:01.790
<v Instructor>Okay, let's conclude this section</v>

2
00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:04.060
with some discussion around different ways

3
00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.350
to do a dating engineering pipeline on AWS.

4
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.810
So for the analytics layer you can have Amazon S3

5
00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.800
and the data can be read by your EMR cluster

6
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.600
that could be running Hadoop, Spark or Hive

7
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.940
and that gives you access to open source Big Data tools

8
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.990
that might be great when you're doing a migration

9
00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.310
from on-premise into AWS.

10
00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.630
If you're using more AWS native tools such as Redshift

11
00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.770
you could Redshift and Redshift Spectrum

12
00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:33.150
to do data warehousing and query the data either directly

13
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.670
on the S3 through Redshift Spectrum or load it first

14
00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.290
into your Redshift warehouse and perform queries there

15
00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.190
using SQL.

16
00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:42.760
And then you have Athena which is going to be serverless

17
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.210
for more sporadic queries and to query data

18
00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.330
directly into S3.

19
00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.930
As we've seen QuickSight is for visualization

20
00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:52.500
and you can integrate easily with Redshift

21
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.810
or Athena to perform some dashboards and create them

22
00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.190
and then use the Redshift or Athena as your internal

23
00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.560
query layer to get your data in the right form.

24
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.940
Okay, now in terms of Big Data ingestion.

25
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.040
What can they look like?

26
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.920
Say you have IoT devices and they produce data in real-time

27
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.840
into Kinesis Data Stream then we could use Kinesis Data

28
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.700
Firehose to ingest the data and send it near real-time

29
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.350
maybe one minute between the ingestion bucket

30
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:22.290
such as Amazon S3 and remember we can use Lambda functions

31
00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.420
to transform the data before it gets into S3

32
00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.040
in case we need to.

33
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.170
Then we could have S3 events, for example,

34
00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.920
to go through an SQS queue as an optional service

35
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.250
or directly into Lambda to notify that a new file

36
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.540
has been inserted into S3.

37
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:43.040
Many all in the function can trigger an Amazon Athena job

38
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.490
which is going to directly query the data

39
00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.180
into our S3 buckets and insert that

40
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.060
into our reporting buckets.

41
00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.290
And then finally, from that reporting bucket

42
00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.360
we could create a QuickSight dashboard

43
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.840
or use Amazon Redshift to create or ingest that data

44
00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.860
and query it.

45
00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.190
So we have different tools it's really mix and match

46
00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.140
in this case, it really depends on the type of job

47
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.070
you're running and the type of architecture

48
00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:05.980
you want to have.

49
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.410
As you can see it's very easy to go from a real-time layer

50
00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:11.370
all the way through a reporting layer

51
00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.270
using the different tools available to you in AWS.

52
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.530
Okay, now if we have to compare the warehousing technologies

53
00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:21.700
I think it's pretty clear but one last time it doesn't hurt

54
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.170
so EMR is going to be be when we need to use Big Data tools

55
00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:29.000
such as Apache Hive or Spark or other things

56
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.967
and migrate that to AWS.

57
00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.330
We have the architraval discussion to use

58
00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:37.540
either one long-running cluster and we'll submit

59
00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.920
many jobs to that cluster and can even enable Hadoop scaling

60
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.450
for that cluster to be scaling based on how many jobs

61
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:47.070
we have pending or we can choose a more clustered

62
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.360
type of architecture when we have one cluster per job

63
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.400
and we can size each job and each cluster independently.

64
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.280
Then you have different purchasing options

65
00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.450
to take into account with EMR to do some cost saving.

66
00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:03.670
So Spot Instances, On Demand Instances, Reserved Instances

67
00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.680
all of these are possible based on the node you're using.

68
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.680
And so it really depends on are you willing to lose nodes,

69
00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.450
are you willing to run a cluster for a year,

70
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.320
these kinds of questions.

71
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.630
An EMR can access data in DynamoDB through Hive

72
00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:22.440
or S3 through EMR FS.

73
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.530
You can use Scratch data on EBS disks and this is HDFS

74
00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:29.834
or use long term storage in S3 through EMR FS

75
00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:31.370
as I just said.

76
00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.490
Athena is going to be for simple queries and aggregations

77
00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.750
and the data must live in Amazon S3.

78
00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:41.110
It is going to be serverless for simple queries SQL

79
00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.760
and you're going to have out of the box queries

80
00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.600
for many services including custom billing reports,

81
00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.020
et cetera, et cetera.

82
00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:51.480
Athena is very well integrated with AWS so you can audit

83
00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.940
all the queries being made through CloudTrail.

84
00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.160
And finally, your Redshift is going to be for Advanced

85
00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.370
SQL queries you must provision servers, you can leverage

86
00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.810
as well a Redshift Spectrum for serverless queries on S3

87
00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.670
and Redshift's going to be really a full data warehouse

88
00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.040
and you're expected some kind of sustained usage

89
00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.080
of Redshift to make your return on investment.

90
00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:13.930
Okay, so that's all for Big Data on AWS.

